      "judge_rationale": "The summary captures the main arguments and examples from the source: the traditional (callous) model of toughness (Bobby Knight examples), Baumrind’s responsiveness/demandingness framework and authoritarian harms (including the 31% stat and 81% public perception), the Jordan McNair case and rhabdomyolysis concerns, the contrast with coaches like Pete Carroll, the 2008 Eastern Washington study, the redefinition of toughness (navigating discomfort), the Toughness Maxims, and discussions of meaning/purpose and post-traumatic growth. These elements justify high concept_coverage, qualifier_preservation, no_fluff, and structure_quality scores. Deduction in faithfulness is for several specific claims or study citations in the candidate that are not substantiated by the provided source excerpt or the source-derived rubric: (a) the detailed personal case framed as 'Paradoxical Vocal Cord Dysfunction (VCD)' with medical characterization and the mile-race anecdote — that specific diagnosis and framing do not appear in the supplied text/rubric; (b) several numerical study references and examples (e.g., “57 NBA coaches,” “64 NCAA track teams,” “a study of 1,000 office workers,” and some precise team/coach anecdotes like Gregg Popovich’s dinners presented as specific empirical findings) that are not present in the provided excerpt/rubric; (c) one or two extra specifics about study outcomes (e.g., technical-foul tracking across careers) not shown in the provided material. Because the evaluator must score only what is supported by the supplied source/rubric, these unsupported additions reduce the faithfulness score despite the summary’s overall accurate and well-structured presentation of the core material.",
